 {:seon.task/name "a-thread-wakes-its-joiners-before-it-leaves-the-live-threads"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-09 by the main-carrier stuck fix (Jolt 869d77f6), left open on purpose rather than patched: a thread's exit (reference-code/jolt/host/chez/lazy-bridge.ss, the exit path near jolt-thread-exit-hook and jolt-thread-end-wake) removes it from live-threads and only after that wakes the threads and fibers joining it, so between the two a deadlock check sees no live thread that may run while a joiner is about to be woken, and can tell that joiner a false deadlock. The patch the fix declined was counting a thread mid-exit as able to run (a special case in the detector). The cause is the order: an ending thread wakes its joiners first and leaves live-threads after, both under the lock the detector reads with, so no check can see the gap. The comment there explains why live-threads is updated before the exit hook (a late parent re-adding an entry); keep that guarantee. Proof, red first: a fiber on the main carrier that joins a thread ending in that window is answered by the join, never told a deadlock (force the window as test/chez/main-carrier-stuck-test.clj forces its)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{jolt.host-test/fiber-main-carrier-joins-thread-ending-exit-window}}
 {:seon.task/name "jolt-is-at-upstreams-head"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner's ruling (docs/decisions.md, 2026-11-10; docs/forks.md#following-upstream). What exists: our fork merged onto upstream main's head (552 upstream commits) on the fork's branch ``jolt/on-upstream-2026-11-10`` (tip `601c9379` on 2026-11-11), re-minted; Jolt's 192 gates run on it one at a time: 178 pass; the 14 red are red on today's pin too except none (hostarity went green); two are red on upstream as well (smoke's and fiberspoll's macOS socket cases). Seon's branch ``jolt/joltpin`` pins it with jolt-lang/time v0.0.15 and seon.sock's typed ``ffi/write-array`` calls. Its whole prove (2026-11-11, with main `20cf1174c0`) is red on: `seon.model-test/a-call-that-names-itself-is-answered-as-a-stream-over-the-socket` and `a-streamed-reply-ends-when-the-server-ends-it` (`seon.fx/read-lines!` handed nil: streaming over a socket); `bin/check idle` (the browser tab's event stream); `bin/check scale` `:root-threads-as-declared` (52 threads on `clojure-agent-send-off-pool`, the `jolt-io-poller` thread 1 to 0: upstream runs futures on a cached pool); `seon.cost-test/the-unit-table-is-current` (``:sorted/assoc`` 838 to 58 ticks and ``:sort/compare`` cheaper; ``:map/reduce-kv`` 1.24 to 2.31 ticks, worse, cause not named); `seon.render-cost-test/a-step-costs-what-changed` (bytes 1.33x at n = 640). All 100 fork units pass on it. Measured: the pin is not a cost change for the store (a warm seek 2-3% fewer ticks, 10% fewer bytes; a write unchanged); a deftype's construction is 2-3x cheaper; the compiler refuses a malformed ns form, an overlapping fn arity and a method declared twice. Not ours to weaken: by the thread law (docs/machine.md) work the old Jolt ran on fibers and the new one runs on OS threads is fixed or configured in the fork, never added to the declared set. Done means: each red fixed at its cause or, for a cost that got better, re-taken with its cause named; `bin/prove` whole green on the pin's own branch, run by the session that brings it; the